## Visual concept

The "Clinical Nocturne" series style (locked from Sleep 1.0), new sub-archetype: **the night dispensary cabinet** in architectural elevation, continuing the spatial story (1.0 building → 2.0 sealed room → 2.1 glide-path → 3.0 the cabinet on the room's wall). Headline = the brief's flagged-strongest provocation, `MELATONIN ISN'T A SLEEPING PILL.` Eye path mirrors the article: a full-width three-latch GATE (`ROOM (2.0)` · `ROUTINE (2.1)` · `TIME 2–3 weeks`, "the 10% margin unlocks only after the free 90%") → Shelf 1 **THE CALMING FOUR** (glycine / magnesium glycinate in warm amber = cools the body; apigenin / L-theanine in cool violet = calms the mind; each with mechanism/dose/best-for spec tags and honest 3-tick evidence meters, glycine fullest; mag carries the `glycinate ✓ · citrate ~ · oxide ✗` form chip) → the **CLOCK DRAWER** pulled out to the right (melatonin as a clock-faced vial, `IT'S A CLOCK SIGNAL.`, dose-inversion bars 3–10 mg ghost vs 0.3–0.5 mg lit, timing-shift axis with dose hours before bedtime, label-lottery −83%→+478% and Malaysia-Rx chips — amber caution, deliberately not red) → Shelf 2 **THE MAYBES** dimmed at reduced scale (ashwagandha with caveats, tart cherry, valerian, lemon balm/lavender, plus dashed never-stocked ghosts for oral GABA and CBD) → the **HAZARD BIN** at the cabinet floor (the poster's only red: antihistamine "PM" pills behind caution-hatch glass, three fault lines, rotated `OCCASIONAL ONLY — NEVER THE NIGHTLY PLAN` stamp) → the **DISPENSED TRAY** (racing-mind stack chips + `T−30–60 MIN` clock chip + ritual caption, connector to the 2.0 bedroom) → escalation footer to Part 3.1. Illumination gradient inside the cabinet (bright top → dim maybes → red-lit floor) encodes evidence confidence. §7 avoided: no supplement flat-lay, no equal-weight grid, melatonin not red-villained.

